---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Copyright (c) 2026 The CIR Authors
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

interface Principle {
  num: number;
  title: string;
  body: string;
  featured?: boolean;
}

// Distilled from /docs/ethos.md. The full text lives in the repo —
// this page summarizes; we link to the source for the canonical version.
const principles: Principle[] = [
  {
    num: 1,
    title: 'Dynamic over static',
    body:
      'UI is composed at runtime by the compiler, not authored as static React pages. A demo that renders the same DOM whether the LLM ran or not is not demonstrating CIR.',
  },
  {
    num: 2,
    title: 'Composition, not invention',
    body:
      'The LLM picks from a registered ComponentBinding catalog. Custom bindings are vocabulary the LLM has access to — they are not pre-baked layouts. The LLM never writes React.',
  },
  {
    num: 3,
    title: 'Intent reshapes, not just decorates',
    body:
      'Different intent profiles must produce structurally different manifests. Lens density is a recompile, not a CSS toggle.',
  },
  {
    num: 4,
    title: 'Policies enforce, not document',
    body:
      'Every policy enforces against the rendered manifest tree. A policy that only fires in tests is not a policy — it is a unit test.',
  },
  {
    num: 5,
    title: 'Visible compilation',
    body:
      'Demos must make the dynamic-UI thesis observable. Token counters tick. Compile time shows. Cache invalidation is visible. The framework machinery is celebrated, not hidden.',
  },
  {
    num: 6,
    title: 'Recipe is the source',
    body:
      'Every demo persona has a recipe declaring intent surfaces, granted capabilities, candidate skills, and policy obligations. Manifest authoring by humans is a smell.',
  },
  {
    num: 7,
    title: 'Schema-validated contracts',
    body:
      'Manifest props are validated against component contracts at the schema layer. Defensive component code is forgiveness for an enforcement gap.',
  },
  {
    num: 8,
    title: 'Capability dispatch is first-class',
    body:
      'Components declare action slots in binding metadata. The renderer maps node.actions[i] to the declared slot. No bespoke per-component capability-prop filters.',
  },
  {
    num: 9,
    title: 'Resolver supplies fallbacks',
    body:
      'Empty / loading / error states come from the resolver pipeline by default. Manifests opt out, not in.',
  },
  {
    num: 10,
    title: 'Demos are existence proofs',
    body:
      'Every demo must demonstrate something only CIR can do. If a demo could be implemented as a static React app with the same UX, it does not belong as a CIR demo.',
  },
  {
    num: 11,
    title: 'The marketplace is the product; custom bindings are a last resort',
    featured: true,
    body:
      'The framework promise is real only if hosts can ship apps without writing per-host React for every domain shape. Each per-host ComponentBinding is a local escape hatch — a place where the host has decided the LLM cannot be trusted to compose the shape from primitives. Most "custom bindings" are smell. The right response to a missing shape is to promote it to baseline. Aurora ships zero. Octant and Marigold followed in the marketplace pivot.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Ethos"
  description="The eleven principles every CIR implementation honors."
  pageKey="ethos"
>
  <h1>Ethos</h1>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>CIR's core thesis:</strong> a UI's <em>layout</em> is a runtime artifact. A compiler
      takes a recipe (declarative app description), the user's intent profile, and the granted
      capabilities, and produces a manifest tree. The runtime walks the tree against a registered
      binding catalog. Policies validate the tree before render.
    </p>
    <p><strong>The human authors recipes; the LLM authors manifests.</strong></p>
  </blockquote>

  <p class="lead">
    Anything that contradicts this thesis — no matter how clean it looks — is drift, and should be
    flagged in review. The eleven principles below are falsifiable; if a PR violates one and there
    is a defensible reason, the reason goes in the commit body.
  </p>

  <h2>The eleven principles</h2>

  {
    principles.map((p) => (
      <section class={p.featured ? 'principle featured' : 'principle'} aria-labelledby={`p-${p.num}`}>
        <h3 id={`p-${p.num}`}>
          <span class="num">#{p.num}</span>
          {p.title}
        </h3>
        <p>{p.body}</p>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <hr />
  <p>
    The full source — including reviewer guidance and a worked example of "what true to ethos
    looks like, concretely" — lives at
    <a
      href="https://github.com/fragmatic-io/cir/blob/main/docs/ethos.md"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      target="_blank">docs/ethos.md</a
    >.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
